sixteen hours after belarusian  opposition leader maria kolesnikova was  abducted on the streets of minsk  a car pulls up at the border with  ukraine it's four am  according to the belarussian version of  events the car sped up  endangering the life of a border guard  before kolesnikova was thrown out of it  by her colleagues  now in kiev they tell a very different  story  they say they were told they would face  criminal prosecution and jail terms  unless they agreed to leave for ukraine  but that kolesnikova had refused to  cross the border that she'd ripped up  her passport saying she was not going  anywhere  in minsk supporters of kolesnikova came  out chanting her name  until true to form balaclavad men in  military uniforms started picking out  protesters one by one  and dragging them away  [Music]  kalesnikov's teammate veronica told us  from warsaw that what is happening in  belarus carries echoes from the nazi  past  it's a heart breaking when you see this  story when you hear the stories when you  see how kashanka tortures people how he  beats them up how is it for me for his  concentration camps  i thought this concentration camp here  was uh  over 70 years ago but apparently in  dallas we still have concentration camps  president lukashenko spoke today with  russian state media ahead of a trip to  moscow in the coming days  i must protect what was created by our  hands protect those people who created  it  as this is the overwhelming majority the  majority voted for me  despite them seeing me every day  everywhere the iron the tv  the refrigerator maybe somewhere they  got bored  but they supported me  [Music]  say she was taken in this convoy from a  military unit near the ukrainian border  the belarusian authorities have not said  where they're taking her  but even as lukashenko picks off the  opposition one by one  the crowds come out determined to keep  the pressure on the leader  they no longer want danny mcnay sky news  moscow
 

